  "sections": {
    "research": {
      "title": "Research",
      "label": "Linguistics & Speech Science",
      "cover": "images/gallery/img-20260611-145657.jpg",
      "intro": "I study Linguistics and Information Science at International Christian University, and from 2026 I will be on exchange at the University of Edinburgh's School of Informatics. My work sits between phonetics, speech science and machine learning.\n\nMost of it concerns Southern Min — Hokkien, Taiwanese — and its checked tones: the abrupt syllables ending in /k/, /p/, /t/ and a glottal stop. They form a clean contrast in the phonology, but acoustically the cues scatter across duration, formant transitions and voice quality, which makes them a good test of what is really separable in the signal. Low-resource languages are where the interesting problems are: there is no large corpus to fall back on, so you have to be honest about what the data can support."
    },
    "faith": {
      "title": "Faith",
      "label": "Testimony & Christian History",
      "cover": "images/gallery/20260208093200-img-9152.jpg",
      "intro": "I came to faith at fourteen and was baptised in 2023. What changed was not that my circumstances improved, but that my worth stopped depending on them.\n\nAt ICU I try to put that into practice rather than hold it privately: serving at Worship Night, hosting the Alpha Course for people with real questions rather than settled answers, and organising the Faith × Research Symposium during C-Week — because I do not think faith and academic work belong in separate rooms. Collected here are testimonies given at church and chapel, alongside historical research on Christianity in Japan and China."
    },
    "opinions": {
      "title": "Opinions",
      "label": "Education, Society & East Asia",
      "cover": "images/gallery/img-20260624-211640.jpg",
      "intro": "Essays on the things I keep returning to: what cramming education does to children in China, how Chinese residents are seen in Japan, the ethics of language policy when a state decides which languages count, and where science and scripture meet.\n\nThe common thread is what happens to people at the edges of systems — of an education system, of a language policy, of a society's idea of who belongs. These are arguments rather than conclusions, and I would rather be disagreed with than ignored."
    },
    "photos": {
      "title": "Photos",
      "label": "Photography",
      "cover": "images/gallery/20260711165907-img-0841.jpg",
      "intro": "I photograph what I walk past — mostly Tokyo and the places I travel to, taken on the way to somewhere else. Ordinary scenes that hold still for a moment: morning light on a wooden hut, blossom over a bicycle rack, Fuji framed by tangled wires. I like the wires. The mountain alone is a postcard; the wires make it a place where someone lives."
    },
